<span class="kicker">2026-08-15 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>How to Lay Down an 18×18 Paver Walkway</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>An 18×18 inch paver is one of the larger standard slab sizes used for walkways, and it behaves a bit differently than the smaller pavers covered in most general walkway guides. Fewer, larger units mean fewer joints, faster coverage per square foot, and a cleaner, more modern look, but the layout math and spacing decisions work a little differently than with smaller format pavers. This size also lends itself well to two distinct styles, a tight-jointed walkway that reads as one continuous surface, or a stepping-stone style layout with visible gaps filled with gravel, mulch, or grass.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, 18×18 pavers are a popular choice for homeowners across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities, who want a cleaner, more contemporary walkway look. This guide covers how to lay this size both ways, tight-jointed and spaced, along with the layout planning specific to a larger format paver.</p>
<h2><b>Tight-Jointed Versus Spaced Layout for 18×18 Pavers</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Factor</b></td><td><b>Tight-Jointed</b></td><td><b>Spaced/Stepping-Stone Style</b></td></tr><tr><td>Appearance</td><td>Continuous, solid surface</td><td>Modern, airy, more casual</td></tr><tr><td>Base needed</td><td>Full compacted gravel base</td><td>Lighter base, sometimes sand-set for light use</td></tr><tr><td>Material between pavers</td><td>Polymeric sand joints</td><td>Gravel, mulch, or turf/groundcover</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>Main walkways, regular daily use</td><td>Garden paths, side yards, accent areas</td></tr><tr><td>Water permeability</td><td>Low</td><td>High, water passes through the gaps</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Both styles use the same basic paver, the difference is entirely in spacing and what fills the gaps between units, which changes both the look and the underlying construction approach.</p>
<h2><b>Planning Your Layout With 18×18 Pavers</b></h2>
<h3><b>Calculating How Many Pavers You Need</b></h3>
<p>An 18×18 inch paver covers 2.25 square feet. For a tight-jointed layout, divide your total walkway square footage by 2.25 to get an approximate paver count, then add 5 to 10 percent for cuts along curves or edges. For a spaced layout, the math depends on your chosen gap width and spacing pattern, since you’re covering the same walkway length with fewer pavers and more open space between them.</p>
<h3><b>Choosing a Walkway Width That Works With This Size</b></h3>
<p>Because 18 inches is a substantial single unit, walkway width often works out cleanly in multiples of that dimension. A 3 foot wide walkway fits two pavers across with a standard joint, while a single-file path might use one paver per row at 18 to 24 inches wide. Planning your width around the paver size, rather than fighting an odd width that requires awkward cuts on every row, saves both material and labor.</p>
<h3><b>Pattern Options for Larger Format Pavers</b></h3>
<p>Larger pavers like 18×18 units generally work best with simpler patterns. A straightforward grid or running bond pattern suits the scale well, while intricate patterns like herringbone are rarely used with pavers this large, since the visual effect that makes herringbone work relies on a smaller, more repetitive unit size.</p>
<h2><b>Building a Tight-Jointed 18×18 Paver Walkway</b></h2>
<h3><b>Step 1: Mark and Excavate</b></h3>
<p>Mark your walkway path and width using stakes and string, then excavate to a depth accounting for base, sand, and paver thickness combined, generally 7 to 9 inches total.</p>
<h3><b>Step 2: Build and Compact the Base</b></h3>
<p>Add crushed gravel in 2 inch layers, compacting thoroughly with a plate compactor after each layer, following the same base depth principles as any tight-jointed paver walkway, 4 to 6 inches depending on soil type.</p>
<h3><b>Step 3: Add Landscape Fabric and Bedding Sand</b></h3>
<p>Roll landscape fabric over the compacted base, then add and screed about 1 inch of coarse sand for a level surface.</p>
<h3><b>Step 4: Lay the Pavers</b></h3>
<p>Place each 18×18 paver carefully, since their larger size and weight make small placement errors more noticeable, and harder to nudge into position, than with smaller pavers. Check level frequently, since a large paver sitting even slightly high or low is much more visible than the same issue with a smaller unit.</p>
<h3><b>Step 5: Cut Border Pavers</b></h3>
<p>Because these pavers are large, edge cuts represent a bigger chunk of material loss than with smaller pavers, so measure carefully before cutting to minimize waste.</p>
<h3><b>Step 6: Install Edge Restraints and Fill Joints</b></h3>
<p>Secure edge restraints around the perimeter, then sweep polymeric sand into the joints between pavers, mist to activate, and run the plate compactor over the finished surface.</p>

